%[text] %[text:anchor:T_nid0] # Estimate Non-Isolated DC-DC Converter Parameters
%[text] This script sizes the power stage and tunes the cascaded controller of a non-isolated DC-DC converter, in any of six topologies: a buck, a boost or a buck-boost, each with a synchronous variant. You specify the voltages, the rated power, the switching frequency, the allowed ripple and the load-transient requirement. It returns the inductance, the output capacitance, the device rating, the conduction mode and both sets of loop gains.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ov] ## Overview
%[text] All six topologies share one inductor, one actively switched device, one rectifying device and one output capacitor. They differ in where the inductor sits, which sets the duty:
%[text] $d\_{\\textrm{buck}} =\\frac{V\_{out} }{V\_{in} }\\;,\\qquad d\_{\\textrm{boost}} =1-\\frac{V\_{in} }{V\_{out} }\\;,\\qquad d\_{\\textrm{buck}-\\textrm{boost}} =\\frac{V\_{out} }{V\_{in} +V\_{out} }$
%[text] The script rejects a specification the topology cannot reach, such as an output voltage below the input range on a boost.
%[text] The script does the following:
%[text] 1. Check that the topology can reach the specified output voltage.
%[text] 2. Size the inductance $L${"editStyle":"visual"} from the allowed ripple.
%[text] 3. Verify the continuous-conduction-mode (CCM) margin and report the power below which conduction goes discontinuous.
%[text] 4. Size the output capacitance $C\_o${"editStyle":"visual"} from the ripple and the load-transient requirement.
%[text] 5. Report the device currents and the device voltage rating.
%[text] 6. Place the inner current loop and the outer voltage loop against that sized power stage. \
%[text] The [Annexure](internal:H_annex) lists the equations and the variable definitions.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_top] ## Specify Topology
%[text] Select one of the six topologies. It must match the **Topology** parameter of the plant and controller blocks.
%[text] A synchronous variant replaces the rectifying diode with a second switch driven complementary to the first. Its inductor current goes negative at light load instead of becoming discontinuous.
nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.topology = "Synchronous Boost"; % "Buck" | "Boost" | "Buck-Boost", each also "Synchronous ..."
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_rate] ## Specify Converter Rating
%[text] Enter the converter ratings. The design uses the whole input voltage range, not only the nominal point.
nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.minInputVoltage = simscape.Value(180,"V"); % Minimum input DC voltage
nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.nomInputVoltage = simscape.Value(200,"V"); % Nominal input DC voltage
nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.maxInputVoltage = simscape.Value(220,"V"); % Maximum input DC voltage

nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.outputVoltage    = simscape.Value(400,"V");  % Regulated output voltage
nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.ratedPower       = simscape.Value(1500,"W"); % Rated (maximum) output power
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_sw] ## Specify Switching Frequency and Allowed Ripple
%[text] These two together set the inductance. The ripple fraction is the peak-to-peak high-frequency ripple as a fraction of the average inductor current.
%[text] The ripple fraction also fixes the conduction mode. Conduction becomes discontinuous below a fixed fraction of rated power:
%[text] $\\frac{P\_{CCM} }{P\_{\\textrm{rated}} }=\\frac{r\_i }{2}\\;,\\qquad \\frac{L}{L\_{CCM} }=\\frac{2}{r\_i }$
%[text] A ripple fraction of $1/3${"editStyle":"visual"} gives discontinuous conduction below one sixth of rated power. Raising the switching frequency does not widen that range.
%[text] The output voltage ripple fraction is also peak-to-peak. It rarely sizes the capacitor, because the load transient in the next section usually governs.
nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.switchingFrequency = simscape.Value(100e3,"Hz"); % Switching frequency
nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.inductorCurrentRippleFraction = 1/3;  % Peak-to-peak ripple as a fraction of average current
nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.outputVoltageRippleFraction   = 0.01; % Peak-to-peak switching ripple (fraction of Vout)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_tran] ## Specify Load Transient Response
%[text] On a load step the output capacitor supplies the deficit until the voltage loop responds. Holding the resulting dip inside a tolerance gives the capacitance:
%[text] $C\_{o,\\textrm{step}} =\\frac{\\Delta I\_{out} }{\\Delta V\_{out} \\cdot 2\\pi f\_c }$
%[text] The voltage-loop crossover frequency is specified here because it sizes the capacitor. The controller design reads it from the design structure.
nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.loadStepFraction                 = 0.50; % Load step as a fraction of rated power
nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.loadStepVoltageDeviationFraction = 0.02; % Allowed output dip (fraction of Vout)
nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.voltageLoopCrossoverFrequency = simscape.Value(200,"Hz"); % Target voltage-loop crossover
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_marg] ## Specify Design Margins
%[text] The conversion ratio margin is how far the output voltage must stay clear of the input range. It must be above 1. The device voltage margin sets the recommended device rating.
%[text] A buck's devices block the maximum input voltage, a boost's block the output voltage, and an inverting buck-boost's block the sum of the two.
nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.conversionRatioMargin = 1.05; % Required clearance of Vout from the input range
nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.deviceVoltageMargin   = 1.30; % Device voltage rating as a multiple of the blocking voltage
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_para] ## Specify Parasitic Parameters
%[text] Use datasheet values. On a boost or a buck-boost the capacitor ESR usually dominates the output voltage ripple.
nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.inductorESR  = simscape.Value(0.05,"Ohm"); % Inductor series resistance
nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.capacitorESR = simscape.Value(0.02,"Ohm"); % Output capacitor series resistance
%[text] The initial output capacitor voltage is left unset, so the design uses the input voltage for a boost and zero otherwise. Set it explicitly to study the startup inrush.
%nonIsolatedDCDCSpec.initialCapacitorVoltage = simscape.Value(200,"V"); % Output capacitor initial voltage
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_est] ## Estimate Power-Stage Parameters
%[text] Compute the inductance, the output capacitance and the conduction mode. `DisplayTableFlag` shows the design table. `SelectNonIsolatedDCDCConverterComponents` gives the device voltage and current ratings.
%[text] `NonIsolatedDCDCDefaultInputParameters` computes the same values, so the model also opens without this script.
[designTable, nonIsolatedDCDCDesign] = designNonIsolatedDCDCConverter(nonIsolatedDCDCSpec, ... %[output:group:a1914871] %[output:a09146de]
    "DisplayTableFlag", true); %[output:group:a1914871] %[output:a09146de]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ripple] ## Verify Inductor Current Ripple and Peak Current
%[text] Plot the inductor current envelope and the realized ripple fraction across the input range.
%[text] The upper edge sizes the core against saturation and the devices against peak current. The lower edge is the CCM boundary.
plotNonIsolatedDCDCInductorCurrentRipple(nonIsolatedDCDCDesign); %[output:199fe50a]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ccm] ## Verify Conduction Mode Across the Input Range
%[text] Requiring $i\_L \>\\Delta I\_L /2${"editStyle":"visual"} at every input voltage gives a boundary in the (input voltage, output power) plane. Above it conduction is continuous, below it discontinuous.
%[text] The power axis is logarithmic, so the plot reads as a fraction of rated power.
plotNonIsolatedDCDCCCMBoundary(nonIsolatedDCDCDesign); %[output:7804a365]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ctrl] ## Specify Controller Parameters
%[text] Tune the cascaded controller against the sized power stage. An inner current PI in per-unit follows the reference from an outer voltage PI. A duty feedforward carries the nominal operating point, and both loops have anti-windup.
%[text] Everything shared with the power stage comes out of `nonIsolatedDCDCDesign`. Every parameter below is optional and falls back to the default shown.
%[text] **Inner current loop.** A PI crossing over at $\\omega\_c${"editStyle":"visual"} with its zero a decade below gives about 84 degrees of phase margin, less the delay of one current sample plus half a switching period, $\\omega\_c \\tau\_d${"editStyle":"visual"} radians.
nonIsolatedDCDCControllerSpec.currentLoopBandwidthRatio = 1/20; % Current-loop crossover as Fsw*ratio
nonIsolatedDCDCControllerSpec.currentLoopZeroRatio      = 1/10; % PI zero as a fraction of that crossover
%[text] **Outer voltage loop.** The plant seen from the current reference is first order, and a PI places both of its closed-loop roots. The damping ratio is therefore a specification, not an outcome.
nonIsolatedDCDCControllerSpec.voltageLoopDampingRatio = 0.8; % Closed-loop damping of the voltage loop
%[text] **Sample times.** The current loop runs once per switching period and the voltage loop a decade slower.
nonIsolatedDCDCControllerSpec.currentSampleTimeRatio      = 1;  % Current loop sample time as ratio*(1/Fsw)
nonIsolatedDCDCControllerSpec.voltageToCurrentSampleRatio = 10; % Voltage loop runs this many times slower
%[text] **Current limit and soft start.** The current limit clamps the voltage loop's output. The floor is zero, because none of these topologies sinks current.
%[text] The ramp removes the inrush of a step reference, and the limit shapes the charging of the output capacitor.
nonIsolatedDCDCControllerSpec.currentLimitPu    = 1.5;  % Current reference upper limit, pu of the inductor current base
nonIsolatedDCDCControllerSpec.currentLimitMinPu = 0;    % Current reference lower limit, pu
nonIsolatedDCDCControllerSpec.softStartTime = simscape.Value(4e-3,"s"); % Reference ramp duration
%[text] **Modulator and gate drive.** Dead time and the duty limits are hardware constraints, not estimated quantities. Dead time costs $T\_d f\_{sw}${"editStyle":"visual"} of duty range at each end and applies only to the synchronous topologies.
nonIsolatedDCDCControllerSpec.deadTime = simscape.Value(100e-9,"s"); % Complementary gate dead time
nonIsolatedDCDCControllerSpec.dutyMax  = 0.95; % Modulator duty upper limit
nonIsolatedDCDCControllerSpec.dutyMin  = 0.02; % Modulator duty lower limit
nonIsolatedDCDCControllerSpec.modulatorSampleTime = simscape.Value(1e-8,"s"); % Carrier rate, ramp mode only

nonIsolatedDCDCControllerSpec.gateVoltageOn  = simscape.Value(15,"V"); % Gate drive ON level
nonIsolatedDCDCControllerSpec.gateVoltageOff = simscape.Value(0,"V");  % Gate drive OFF level
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ctrlest] ## Estimate Controller Parameters
%[text] Place both loops. The achieved natural frequency and damping in the table are recomputed from the final gains, so a gain moved by a limit shows up in the achieved column.
[controllerTable, nonIsolatedDCDCController] = designNonIsolatedDCDCController( ... %[output:group:a0aed375] %[output:9faed1e2]
    nonIsolatedDCDCDesign, nonIsolatedDCDCControllerSpec, "DisplayTableFlag", true); %[output:group:a0aed375] %[output:9faed1e2]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_annex] ## Annexure: Equations and Definitions
%[text] **Conversion feasibility and duty.** From the CCM volt-second balance, with the duty required to lie inside $\\left(0,1\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} across the input range:
%[text] $d\_{\\textrm{buck}} =\\frac{V\_{out} }{V\_{in} }\\;,\\qquad d\_{\\textrm{boost}} =1-\\frac{V\_{in} }{V\_{out} }\\;,\\qquad d\_{\\textrm{buck}-\\textrm{boost}} =\\frac{V\_{out} }{V\_{in} +V\_{out} }$
%[text] A buck therefore needs $V\_{out} \<V\_{in,\\min } /\\textrm{margin}${"editStyle":"visual"} and a boost needs $V\_{out} \>\\textrm{margin}\\cdot V\_{in,\\max }${"editStyle":"visual"} . A buck-boost has no such restriction.
%[text] **Average inductor current.** A buck's inductor carries the output current, a boost's carries the input current, and a buck-boost's carries both in turn:
%[text] $i\_{L,\\textrm{buck}} =\\frac{P}{V\_{out} }\\;,\\qquad i\_{L,\\textrm{boost}} =\\frac{P}{V\_{in} }\\;,\\qquad i\_{L,\\textrm{buck}-\\textrm{boost}} =\\frac{P}{V\_{in} }+\\frac{P}{V\_{out} }$
%[text] **Inductance.** The ripple is the on-time volt-second product divided by the inductance. Holding it inside $r\_i i\_{L,\\textrm{avg}}${"editStyle":"visual"} across the input range gives:
%[text] $\\Delta I\_L =\\frac{\\lambda\_{\\textrm{on}} }{L}\\;,\\qquad \\lambda\_{\\textrm{on},\\textrm{buck}} =\\frac{\\left(V\_{in} -V\_{out} \\right)d}{f\_{sw} }\\;,\\qquad \\lambda\_{\\textrm{on},\\textrm{boost}} =\\lambda\_{\\textrm{on},\\textrm{bb}} =\\frac{V\_{in} d}{f\_{sw} }\\;,\\qquad L=\\max\_{V\_{in} } \\frac{\\lambda\_{\\textrm{on}} }{r\_i i\_{L,\\textrm{avg}} }$
%[text] **Continuous conduction.** Requiring $i\_{L,\\textrm{avg}} \>\\Delta I\_L /2${"editStyle":"visual"} with the sized $L${"editStyle":"visual"} above gives two identities containing neither $f\_{sw}${"editStyle":"visual"} , $L${"editStyle":"visual"} nor the power level:
%[text] $\\frac{L}{L\_{CCM} }=\\frac{2}{r\_i }\\;,\\qquad \\frac{P\_{CCM} }{P\_{\\textrm{rated}} }=\\frac{r\_i }{2}$
%[text] **Output capacitance.** Two requirements, and the larger governs. The switching-ripple term differs by topology, because a buck's inductor feeds the output continuously and a boost's or a buck-boost's feeds it in pulses:
%[text] $C\_{o,\\textrm{ripple},\\textrm{buck}} =\\frac{\\Delta I\_L }{8f\_{sw} \\Delta V\_{out} }\\;,\\qquad C\_{o,\\textrm{ripple},\\textrm{boost}} =\\frac{I\_{out} d}{f\_{sw} \\Delta V\_{out} }\\;,\\qquad C\_{o,\\textrm{step}} =\\frac{\\Delta I\_{out} }{\\Delta V\_{out} \\cdot 2\\pi f\_c }$
%[text] **Output ripple, including ESR.** Recomputed with the capacitance chosen. The sum is an upper bound, because the two terms do not peak at the same instant:
%[text] $\\Delta V\_{out} =\\frac{I\_{out} d}{C\_o f\_{sw} }+R\_{co} I\_{L,pk} \\quad \\left(\\textrm{boost},\\;\\textrm{buck}-\\textrm{boost}\\right)\\;,\\qquad \\Delta V\_{out} =\\frac{\\Delta I\_L }{8C\_o f\_{sw} }+R\_{co} \\Delta I\_L \\quad \\left(\\textrm{buck}\\right)$
%[text] **Device and capacitor currents.** The duty splits the inductor current between the two devices, which gives the $\\sqrt{d}${"editStyle":"visual"} scaling. The ripple is left out of these RMS integrals:
%[text] $I\_{Q,rms} =i\_{L,\\textrm{avg}} \\sqrt{d}\\;,\\qquad I\_{D,rms} =i\_{L,\\textrm{avg}} \\sqrt{1-d}\\;,\\qquad I\_{co,rms} =\\frac{\\Delta I\_L }{2\\sqrt{3}}\\;\\left(\\textrm{buck}\\right)\\;,\\qquad I\_{co,rms} =I\_{out} \\sqrt{\\frac{d}{1-d}}\\;\\left(\\textrm{boost}\\right)$
%[text] **Device voltage rating.** What a device blocks is a topology property:
%[text] $V\_{\\textrm{block},\\textrm{buck}} =V\_{in,\\max } \\;,\\qquad V\_{\\textrm{block},\\textrm{boost}} =V\_{out} \\;,\\qquad V\_{\\textrm{block},\\textrm{bb}} =V\_{in,\\max } +V\_{out}$
%[text] ### Controller equations
%[text] **Inner current loop.** In per-unit the inductor current responds to duty as a pure integrator whose gain is the voltage the duty commutates. That voltage is the input for a buck, the output for a boost, and the sum for a buck-boost:
%[text] $\\frac{i\_{L,pu} }{d}=\\frac{V\_d }{sLI\_{\\textrm{base}} }\\;\\Longrightarrow \\;K\_{p,i} =\\frac{\\omega\_c LI\_{\\textrm{base}} }{V\_d }\\;,\\qquad K\_{i,i} =K\_{p,i} \\omega\_c \\cdot \\textrm{zeroRatio}$
%[text] The ideal phase margin follows from the zero placement alone. It is spent on the delay of one current sample plus half a switching period:
%[text] $\\varphi\_{\\textrm{ideal}} =\\arctan \\frac{1}{\\textrm{zeroRatio}}\\;,\\qquad \\varphi =\\varphi\_{\\textrm{ideal}} -\\omega\_c \\left(T\_{s,i} +\\frac{1}{2f\_{sw} }\\right)$
%[text] **Outer voltage loop.** The load-sensitivity exponent $m=\\left(V\_{out} /P\\right)\\partial P/\\partial V\_{out}${"editStyle":"visual"} at fixed inductor current is what makes one set of formulas cover all three families:
%[text] $m\_{\\textrm{buck}} =1\\;,\\qquad m\_{\\textrm{boost}} =0\\;,\\qquad m\_{\\textrm{bb}} =\\frac{V\_{in} }{V\_{in} +V\_{out} }$
%[text] With $V\_{iL} =V\_{out} ,V\_{in} ,V\_{in} V\_{out} /\\left(V\_{in} +V\_{out} \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} respectively, the first-order plant seen from the current reference and the PI that places both of its closed-loop roots exactly are:
%[text] $K=\\frac{V\_{iL} RI\_{\\textrm{base}} }{V\_{out} V\_{\\textrm{base}} \\left(2-m\\right)}\\;,\\qquad \\tau =\\frac{RC}{2-m}\\;,\\qquad K\_{p,v} =\\frac{2\\zeta \\omega\_n \\tau -1}{K}\\;,\\qquad K\_{i,v} =\\frac{\\omega\_n^2 \\tau }{K}$
%[text] The proportional gain goes negative when the requested bandwidth falls below the plant pole. The design then drops it to zero and reports the minimum crossover frequency that avoids it.
%[text] **Soft start against the current limit.** The output capacitor charging time if the current limit is never reached:
%[text] $t\_{\\textrm{unlimited}} =\\frac{C\_o V\_{out} \\left(V\_{out} -V\_{co} \\right)}{V\_{in} I\_{\\lim } -P\_{\\textrm{load}} }$
%[text] **Not sized here.** The dead time and the duty limits are hardware constraints, taken as given. Junction temperature, device data and thermal parameters are chosen in `SelectNonIsolatedDCDCConverterComponents`.
%[text] ### Variable Definitions
%[text] $\\begin{array}{l}\nV\_{in} ,V\_{out} -\\textrm{Input}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{regulated}\\;\\textrm{output}\\;\\textrm{voltage}\\\\\nP-\\textrm{Rated}\\;\\textrm{output}\\;\\textrm{power}\\\\\nd-\\textrm{Main}-\\textrm{switch}\\;\\textrm{duty}\\;\\textrm{in}\\;\\textrm{CCM}\\\\\nf\_{sw} ,T\_{s,i} ,T\_{s,v} -\\textrm{Switching}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{the}\\;\\textrm{two}\\;\\textrm{loop}\\;\\textrm{sample}\\;\\textrm{times}\\\\\nL,C\_o -\\textrm{Inductance}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{output}\\;\\textrm{capacitance}\\\\\nR\_L ,R\_{co} -\\textrm{Inductor}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{capacitor}\\;\\textrm{series}\\;\\textrm{resistance}\\\\\nR,V\_{co} -\\textrm{Load}\\;\\textrm{resistance}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{initial}\\;\\textrm{capacitor}\\;\\textrm{voltage}\\\\\ni\_{L,\\textrm{avg}} ,\\Delta I\_L ,I\_{L,pk} -\\textrm{Average},\\;\\textrm{peak}-\\textrm{to}-\\textrm{peak}\\;\\textrm{ripple}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{peak}\\;\\textrm{inductor}\\;\\textrm{current}\\\\\nr\_i ,r\_v -\\textrm{Allowed}\\;\\textrm{inductor}\\;\\textrm{current}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{output}\\;\\textrm{voltage}\\;\\textrm{ripple}\\;\\textrm{fractions}\\\\\nm-\\textrm{Load}-\\textrm{sensitivity}\\;\\textrm{exponent}\\;\\textrm{of}\\;\\textrm{the}\\;\\textrm{outer}-\\textrm{loop}\\;\\textrm{plant}\\\\\nV\_d ,V\_{iL} -\\textrm{Duty}-\\textrm{to}-\\textrm{inductor}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{inductor}-\\textrm{to}-\\textrm{output}\\;\\textrm{coupling}\\;\\textrm{voltages}\\\\\nI\_{\\textrm{base}} ,V\_{\\textrm{base}} -\\textrm{Per}-\\textrm{unit}\\;\\textrm{inductor}\\;\\textrm{current}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{voltage}\\;\\textrm{bases}\\\\\n\\omega\_c ,\\omega\_n ,\\zeta -\\textrm{Crossover},\\;\\textrm{natural}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{damping}\\;\\textrm{ratio}\\\\\nI\_{\\lim } ,T\_d -\\textrm{Current}\\;\\textrm{limit}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{gate}\\;\\textrm{dead}\\;\\textrm{time}\n\\end{array}$
%%
