%   data: {"dataType":"text","outputData":{"text":"                 <strong>Synchronous Boost Controller Design<\/strong>              \n    <strong>______________________________________________________________<\/strong>\n\n                     <strong>Parameter<\/strong>                      <strong>Value<\/strong>     <strong>Unit<\/strong>\n    <strong>___________________________________________<\/strong>    <strong>_______<\/strong>    <strong>____<\/strong>\n                                                                  \n    Voltage loop Kp (KpVoltage)                         38    -   \n    Voltage loop Ki (KiVoltage)                      31416    -   \n    Voltage loop sample time (TsVoltage)               100    us  \n    Voltage loop crossover (requested)                 200    Hz  \n    Voltage loop natural frequency (achieved)          200    Hz  \n    Voltage loop damping ratio (achieved)              0.8    -   \n    Voltage loop settling to 1% (no soft start)     4.5757    ms  \n    Output plant DC gain (per-unit)                    0.5    -   \n    Output plant pole frequency                         16    Hz  \n    Load sensitivity exponent m                          0    -   \n    Current reference upper limit (iRefMaxPu)          1.5    pu  \n    Current reference lower limit (iRefMinPu)            0    pu  \n    Current reference upper limit, in amps           11.25    A   \n    Soft-start time                                      4    ms  \n    Soft-start time for unlimited charging          19.894    ms  \n    Current loop Kp (KpCurrent)                    0.25659    -   \n    Current loop Ki (KiCurrent)                      806.1    -   \n    Current loop sample time (TsCurrent)                10    us  \n    Current loop crossover                            5000    Hz  \n    Current loop PI zero                               500    Hz  \n    Current loop phase margin (ideal)               84.289    deg \n    Current loop phase lost to delay                    27    deg \n    Current loop phase margin (with delay)          57.289    deg \n    Duty-to-inductor-voltage gain                      400    V   \n    Loop separation (current \/ voltage)                 25    -   \n    Switching frequency (Fsw)                          100    kHz \n    Dead time (Tdead)                                  100    ns  \n    Dead time as fraction of period                   0.01    -   \n    Maximum duty (dutyMax)                            0.95    -   \n    Minimum duty (dutyMin)                            0.02    -   \n    Duty headroom above operating range                0.4    -   \n    Duty headroom below operating range               0.43    -   \n    Modulator sample time (Ts)                          10    ns  \n    Gate drive on voltage                               15    V   \n    Gate drive off voltage                               0    V   \n    Input voltage base (VbaseIn)                       200    V   \n    Output voltage base (VbaseOut)                     400    V   \n    Power base (Pbase)                                1500    W   \n    Inductor current base (IbaseL)                     7.5    A   \n\n\nSynchronous Boost cascaded controller.\n  Current loop crosses at 5000 Hz, one 20 of the switching frequency. The per-unit plant is a pure integrator, so the ideal phase margin is 84.3 deg from the zero placement alone; sampling and the modulator take 27 deg of that, leaving 57.3 deg.\n  Output plant: DC gain 0.5 pu, pole at 16 Hz, from m = 0.\n    m = 0: throughput is set by the input alone, so the stage is a power source and the pole sits at 2\/(R*C) - twice as fast as a buck's, with half the DC gain.\n  Voltage loop places both roots at 200 Hz with damping 0.8, settling inside 1% in 4.576 ms once the soft start has finished.\n  Loop separation 25, and 50 voltage samples per closed-loop period.\n  Current limit 1.5 pu = 11.25 A. Charging the 186.5 uF output capacitor from 200 V to 400 V within that limit alone would take 19.89 ms.\n    The 4 ms soft start is shorter than that, so the current limit shapes the charging profile. That is what the limit is for; the ramp's job here is to remove the initial inrush, not to do the charging.\n  Dead time 100 ns, 1% of the switching period, on the complementary gate pair.\n","truncated":false}}
